{"name":"Coral Coast","hex":"#068e9e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coral","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many species of marine invertebrates in the class Anthozoa, most of which build hard calcium carbonate skeletons and form colonies, or a colony belonging to one of those species.","A hard substance made of the skeletons of these organisms."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of coral.","Having the orange-pink color of coral."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coral"},{"term":"coast","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The edge of the land where it meets an ocean, sea, gulf, bay, or large lake. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The side or edge of something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To glide along without adding energy; to allow a vehicle to continue moving forward after disengaging the engine or ceasing to apply motive power.","To sail along a coast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coral Coast, Western Australia","blocks":[{"text":"Coral Coast is a tourism promotion name for the coastal region along Western Australia's mid western coastline between Cervantes and Exmouth."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_Coast%2C_Western_Australia","wikidata":"Q5169451"},{"title":"Coral Sea","blocks":[{"text":"The Coral Sea is a marginal sea of the South Pacific off the northeast coast of Australia, and classified as an interim Australian bioregion. The Coral Sea extends 2,000 kilometres (1,200 mi) down the Australian north-east coast to latitude 30° south. Most of it is protected between the French Natural Park of the Coral Sea and the Australian Coral Sea Marine Park. The sea was the location for the Battle of the Coral Sea, a major confrontation during World War II between the navies of the Empire of Japan, and the United States and Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Coral_Sea_map.png/330px-Coral_Sea_map.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_Sea","wikidata":"Q82931"},{"title":"Coral","blocks":[{"text":"Corals are colonial marine invertebrates within the subphylum Anthozoa of the phylum Cnidaria. They typically form compact colonies of many identical individual polyps. Coral species include the important reef builders that inhabit tropical oceans and secrete calcium carbonate to form a hard skeleton."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg/330px-Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral","wikidata":"Q171446"},{"title":"Coast","blocks":[{"text":"A coast is the land next to the sea or the line that forms the boundary between the land and the ocean or a lake. Coasts are influenced by the topography of the surrounding landscape and by aquatic erosion, such as that caused by waves. The geological composition of rock and soil dictates the type of shore that is created. Earth has about 620,000.0 km (385,250.1 mi) of coastline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg/330px-Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast","wikidata":"Q93352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Coral_Sea_Islands.png/250px-Coral_Sea_Islands.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Coral_Sea_Islands.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Coral_Sea_Islands.png/250px-Coral_Sea_Islands.png?utm_source=en.wi